  "answer_markdown": "For search and web crawling, I conditionally recommend evaluating **Firecrawl** first, a predominantly TypeScript component: the supplied README read dated 2026-10-11 confirms web search with full result content and extraction into Markdown/JSON. This supports the choice for preparing RAG input documents, but quality and compatibility with your application have not been verified. **Firecrawl MCP Server** has a separate role—it exposes Firecrawl tools to an MCP client; adding it makes sense only if that interface is needed. The README describes hosted keyless scrape/search/parse with rate limits, while crawl/map/agent require a key; the absence of a key does not mean standalone operation. Sources: [Firecrawl](https://github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl), [Firecrawl MCP Server](https://github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl-mcp-server).\n\nProposed flow: query or URL → Firecrawl directly or through the MCP adapter → Markdown/JSON → team-owned validation, deduplication, document chunking and RAG index. In the cloud option, queries and URLs cross an external service boundary; private context is not included in this design. The inferred product benefit is a unified interface for obtaining content for subsequent document search; improvement in RAG quality has not been measured. Duplicates from secondary categories should be removed by repository identity: Firecrawl appears once, while MCP Server remains a separate adapter. This does not prove web-content deduplication: the team still owns URL and document identity, data provenance, reprocessing, queues, errors and quality criteria.\n\nComplexity depends on the application's language and platform, workload, hosting mode, dependencies and maintenance. According to the supplied README, the Firecrawl core is predominantly AGPL-3.0, while SDKs and some UI components use MIT; the cloud includes additional capabilities, and self-hosting equivalence has not been established. [Source](https://github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl). For equivalent scope, a custom implementation must cover search, page retrieval and cleaning, result formats, integration and operations; the existing/native solution should be compared against the same requirements, but the current path is unknown. Savings have not been established. The cards belong to the 2026-09-01 snapshot; the supplied source reads dated 2026-10-11 do not update eligibility: the candidates remain `reference_only`. The order reflects host preference, rather than proof of capabilities or compatibility. The pack is `limited`, `truncated`: coverage of six candidates with IDs 651517185, 905004409, 1163850095, 881458615, 90356372, 895872037 has been lost; they are not recommended. Unknown or truncated fields do not mean capabilities are absent.\n\nScenario `CP04-HOLD-12-first-validation`: a hypothetical budget for first validation only—**4–8 engineer-hours**, low confidence, one engineer familiar with the target application and its component interface, at 8 hours per engineer-day. Before integration, the workload, existing path, language/platform, chosen version and license must be confirmed. Only an isolated synthetic fixture or source-qualification exercise is proposed: no credentials, paid provider, production data or installation. The concrete check is to compare the documented search and Markdown/JSON contract with the application's requirements and use synthetic results to define rules for preserving provenance and removing duplicates. An unacceptable license or a missing required capability in the selected mode would reverse the preference for Firecrawl. Full adoption effort and elapsed working time remain unknown. Because the current path is unknown, the trial should remain isolated, without changing authoritative data or queues; stopping it preserves the ability to continue the previous path. All steps have status `proposed_not_executed`; integration requires a separate explicit request.",
